will mask everything
but the last four digits

00:20:20.354 --> 00:20:22.920
of Social Security numbers,
and fully redact other things

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:24.060
like credit card numbers.

00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:26.490
So something that simple--

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:29.160
again, as you're typing,
we can apply that policy.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:31.576
And you have full control over
what the policy looks like,

00:20:31.576 --> 00:20:34.400
what you look for,
and things like that.

00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:36.730
And-- Good.

00:20:36.730 --> 00:20:40.530
I think-- let's switch
back over to the slides.

00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:41.030
Great.

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:42.500
So that's kind of
applying it in real time.

00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:45.010
So you imagine you could build
that directly into your chat

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:47.260
support app, feedback app,
things like that that might

00:20:47.260 --> 00:20:48.760
be collecting data
where you don't

00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:51.905
expect it to have this certain
sensitive data in there.

00:20:51.905 --> 00:20:53.530
And like we did
before, let's just dive

00:20:53.530 --> 00:20:55.363
a little into the details
of what that might

00:20:55.363 --> 00:20:57.100
look like on the API side.

00:20:57.100 --> 00:20:59.780
So again, we're going to
look at an API call here.

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:03.160
And in this case, we're
not scanning cloud storage.

00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:05.680
So we didn't give
it a bucket to scan.

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:08.270
But instead, we actually
streamed the data in.

00:21:08.270 --> 00:21:11.980
So here's an example of just
streaming a string of text in.

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:14.500
We've asked it to look for
Social Security numbers

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:16.117
and apply a masking transform.

00:21:16.117 --> 00:21:18.700
And then for other things like
credit card number, just simply

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:19.200
redact.

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:20.741
So again this is
showing a little bit

00:21:20.741 --> 00:21:22.840
of what that transformer
de-identification

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:27.880
policy might look like or
the de-identification config.

00:21:27.880 --> 00:21:30.640
And we applied this to
something that we built just

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:31.960
as a little simple app--

00:21:31.960 --> 00:21:33.815
like an App Engine example.

00:21:33.815 --> 00:21:35.690
But if you think about
it, it's in real time.

00:21:35.690 --> 00:21:37.380
So as you have data
in other systems,

00:21:37.380 --> 00:21:39.040
or you have third
party data that

00:21:39.040 --> 00:21:41.170
has APIs where you
can use this data,

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:43.300
you can also build this
into other systems.

00:21:43.300 --> 00:21:46.970
So we've played around in
this a little bit and built--

00:21:46.970 --> 00:21:49.225
like in this example,
we built a Slack bot

00:21:49.225 --> 00:21:50.600
that we kind of
went in and said,

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:52.930
hey, let's see if we can
use the APIs from Slack,

00:21:52.930 --> 00:21:56.350
build a simple Slack bot, stream
that data through our API.

00:21:56.350 --> 00:21:58.087
And we set up this
demo chat room,

00:21:58.087 --> 00:21:59.920
and we kind of put some
information in there

00:21:59.920 --> 00:22:02.507
and then ran a command
and said, what's there?

00:22:02.507 --> 00:22:05.090
So in this case, you can see you
can build it in a third party

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:07.480
so you can kind of use
it on data virtually

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:10.870
anywhere, whether it's a real
time case like this, or data

00:22:10.870 --> 00:22:13.720
that you just manage this
off GCP or in some system

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:15.946
that you control.

00:22:15.946 --> 00:22:17.820
And next, we're going
to dive a little deeper

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:19.984
into de-identification
and risk analysis.

00:22:19.984 --> 00:22:21.650
And my colleague here,
Michael, is going

00:22:21.650 --> 00:22:23.760
to go and present that for you.

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:25.083
And here you go, Michael.

00:22:25.083 --> 00:22:26.291
MICHAEL DAUB: [CLEARS THROAT]

00:22:26.291 --> 00:22:30.027
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:32.015 --> 00:22:33.030
Thanks, Scott.

00:22:33.030 --> 00:22:34.530
So my name is Michael Daub.

00:22:34.530 --> 00:22:38.040
I'm a software engineer
working on cloud DLP.

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:40.260
And so for the second
half of the demo,

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:42.720
I'm going to talk about some
of the more advanced features

00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:45.630
that we offer with the
DLP API, specifically

00:22:45.630 --> 00:22:48.270
de-identification
and risk analysis

00:22:48.270 --> 00:22:51.800
of large structured data.

00:22:51.800 --> 00:22:55.670
So recall in the
last demo, Scott

00:22:55.670 --> 00:22:58.190
showed you how to
de-identify these chat

00:22:58.190 --> 00:23:01.160
messages in real time to make
sure the agent doesn't see it

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:02.810
and that we're
not persisting it.

00:23:02.810 --> 00:23:05.527
But you might imagine
that we've already

00:23:05.527 --> 00:23:07.610
collected these messages,
and we haven't performed

00:23:07.610 --> 00:23:08.600
de-identification yet.

00:23:08.600 --> 00:23:10.640
We're already storing
them in BigQuery,

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:14.030
and we need to go back and
make sure we eliminate any PII

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:15.440
that we don't actually need.

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:17.300
Alternatively, you can
imagine a situation

00:23:17.300 --> 00:23:21.880
where there is sensitive
data that serves

00:23:21.880 --> 00:23:24.690
a legitimate business purpose.

00:23:24.690 --> 00:23:28.180
But some internal
team needs access

00:23:28.180 --> 00:23:30.980
to the data for analysis,
but they don't need access

00:23:30.980 --> 00:23:33.720
to the sensitive data.

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:35.960
And so in either
of these cases, we

00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:38.150
need to take this data
that lives in BigQuery,

00:23:38.150 --> 00:23:40.250
de-identify it just like
in the previous demo,

00:23:40.250 --> 00:23:42.587
and then write it back to
BigQuery so a copy of it

00:23:42.587 --> 00:23:43.920
can be shared with the analysts.

00:23:46.741 --> 00:23:48.490
So it's going to look
very similar to what

00:23:48.490 --> 00:23:50.260
we did before, except
the data, instead

00:23:50.260 --> 00:23:52.150
of originating in
the chat app, is

00:23:52.150 --> 00:23:55.187
going to originate in BigQuery.

00:23:55.187 --> 00:23:56.770
Then we're going to
send it to the DLP

00:23:56.770 --> 00:24:01.240
API for de-identification and
then write it back to BigQuery,

00:24:01.240 --> 00:24:03.560
and do the exact same
transformations we did before.

00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:05.800
Except this time, instead
of doing it in real time,

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:09.550
we're going to do it in batch.

00:24:09.550 --> 00:24:12.270
And so we're going to use
Cloud Data Flow to orchestrate

00:24:12.270 --> 00:24:16.710
the workflow that reads
the data from BigQuery,

00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:20.130
calls the DLP API, and then
writes it back to BigQuery.

00:24:20.130 --> 00:24:23.790
And so we're going to use some
open source code that Google

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:25.050
published to GitHub.

00:24:25.050 --> 00:24:27.420
Again, at the end
of the session,

00:24:27.420 --> 00:24:31.440
we'll provide you with a
link to the GitHub project

00:24:31.440 --> 00:24:35.010
so that you can run the code
on your own BigQuery table

00:24:35.010 --> 00:24:36.480
if you like.

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:39.150
And so right now, we're not
going to run Data Flow live.

00:24:39.150 --> 00:24:41.020
We're going to do
that in a moment.

00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:43.780
So we're just going to
skip over to the results.

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:46.860
And so if you take a
look, the top section

00:24:46.860 --> 00:24:50.610
is the data before we
actually applied DID,

00:24:50.610 --> 00:24:52.440
and the bottom is after.

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:55.890
And so first, I'm going to call
your attention to the User ID

00:24:55.890 --> 00:24:56.920
field.

00:24:56.920 --> 00:24:59.595
So in the top, before
we de-identified it,

00:24:59.595 --> 00:25:02.020
it's just the
sequential user ID,

00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:04.050
which is the raw
user ID that we don't

00:25:04.050 --> 00:25:09.225
want to share with the analysts
because they might have access

00:25:09.225 --> 00:25:12.120
to some other data source that
then they could join on this

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:13.200
using the user ID column.

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:14.910
And so we don't want
them to have that.

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:18.780
But we might want
to at a later time,

00:25:18.780 --> 00:25:21.660
take any analysis they've
done and figure out

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:23.767
who the person is.

00:25:23.767 --> 00:25:25.350
So instead of just
redacting it, we're

00:25:25.350 --> 00:25:28.320
going to use format
preserving encryption

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:31.110
where we're going to
apply cryptography

00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:33.780
using a private
key that can then

00:25:33.780 --> 00:25:35.370
be used later to reverse it.

00:25:35.370 --> 00:25:39.790
And in the output, it just
looks like some random number.

00:25:39.790 --> 00:25:42.480
But it's actually encrypted
and can be reversed.

00:25:44.990 --> 00:25:47.240
And then in the
chat messages, we're

00:25:47.240 --> 00:25:52.940
going to scan for, in this
case, the customer service agent

00:25:52.940 --> 00:25:55.250
names, as well as Social
Security numbers and some

00:25:55.250 --> 00:25:56.990
of the other PII and
then just replace

00:25:56.990 --> 00:25:59.920
them with the type
of PII that was found

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:03.470
so that the analysts will know
what type of PII is missing,

00:26:03.470 --> 00:26:05.090
but not the actual
sensitive contents.

00:26:07.730 --> 00:26:13.160
And so the technical details,
It's the exact same API request

00:26:13.160 --> 00:26:15.560
that Scott showed before.

00:26:15.560 --> 00:26:17.960
I'm just going to
highlight some other--

00:26:17.960 --> 00:26:20.457
call your attention to
some other parts of it.

00:26:20.457 --> 00:26:22.040
Just like before,
we are going to list

00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:24.080
some built in system
defined info types that

00:26:24.080 --> 00:26:26.360
are available to everybody.

00:26:26.360 --> 00:26:28.880
But you can imagine
that you can't expect--

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:30.560
you wouldn't expect
the DLP API to know

00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:32.960
about specific
types that are only

00:26:32.960 --> 00:26:36.110
going to exist in your
data set because they're

00:26:36.110 --> 00:26:37.610
specific to your business.

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:41.437
For example, the custom
case number, in this case,

00:26:41.437 --> 00:26:43.520
is just going to be two
uppercase letters followed

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:45.800
by six digits.

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:48.050
So using our custom
Regex detector,

00:26:48.050 --> 00:26:50.840
you can actually
define your own type.

00:26:50.840 --> 00:26:54.620
And then for the
custom agent name,

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:57.320
we just provide a list
of all the customer

00:26:57.320 --> 00:27:00.230
service agents that are
employed by the company

00:27:00.230 --> 00:27:04.070
in a dictionary that lives in
a file in Google Cloud Storage.

00:27:04.070 --> 00:27:05.690
And then if you
just provide that,

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:07.220
the API will look
up the dictionary

00:27:07.220 --> 00:27:13.360
and then search for those
custom agent names in the data.

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:17.500
And then in terms of the
de-identification config--

00:27:17.500 --> 00:27:20.080
so for the user
ID, here we've just

00:27:20.080 --> 00:27:24.460
defined the format
preserving encryption

00:27:24.460 --> 00:27:28.420
with the provided key to
encrypt those user IDs,

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:30.220
and then redact everything
else, replace it

00:27:30.220 --> 00:27:33.880
with the name of the info type.

00:27:33.880 --> 00:27:37.184
So now suppose that you're not
sharing with an internal team.

00:27:37.184 --> 00:27:39.600
You actually need to share it
with some external marketing

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:41.672
firm to perform
further analysis.

00:27:41.672 --> 00:27:44.130
In this case, we need to take
a bit more care because we're

00:27:44.130 --> 00:27:47.460
sharing it with a team that
might be a little less trusted

00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:48.987
than an internal team.

00:27:48.987 --> 00:27:50.820
And so we've already
taken the obvious steps

00:27:50.820 --> 00:27:54.180
of redacting what are called
direct identifiers, things that

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:57.460
will always obviously
identify a user like user ID,

00:27:57.460 --> 00:27:59.810
social security
number, and so forth.

00:27:59.810 --> 00:28:01.560
But since we want to
take a bit more care,

00:28:01.560 --> 00:28:04.120
what else do we
have to worry about?

00:28:04.120 --> 00:28:06.780
So there's this concept
called quasi identifiers,

00:28:06.780 --> 00:28:11.310
which are identifiers that may
not directly identify somebody.

00:28:11.310 --> 00:28:14.520
If you recall the example Scott
gave earlier of job title,

00:28:14.520 --> 00:28:16.740
for the most part, job
title won't directly

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:17.850
identify somebody.

00:28:17.850 --> 00:28:21.384
But CEO of Google
certainly would.

00:28:21.384 --> 00:28:22.800
In this case, we're
actually going

00:28:22.800 --> 00:28:24.540
to look at zip code and age.

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:28.654
Which any particular zip
code and any particular age,

00:28:28.654 --> 00:28:30.570
there are probably going
to be multiple people

00:28:30.570 --> 00:28:34.350
in your data set for a
given zip code or age.

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:37.110
But actually, quasi
identifiers can be identifying

00:28:37.110 --> 00:28:40.050
when taken together.

00:28:40.050 --> 00:28:42.870
A specific combination
of zip code and age

00:28:42.870 --> 00:28:46.590
might uniquely identify a user
just because the zip code might

00:28:46.590 --> 00:28:48.120
have a low population,
and the age

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:52.390
might be a demographic with
very low population as well.

00:28:55.340 --> 00:28:57.740
And so in this sample
data set, observe

00:28:57.740 --> 00:29:01.247
that there might be five
people of this zip code,

00:29:01.247 --> 00:29:03.080
maybe there are more
that we're not showing.

00:29:03.080 --> 00:29:05.930
But there could be one
person who's age is 81,

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:08.460
and that person would
be highly identifying.

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:11.450
And so we want to make
sure that we're not

00:29:11.450 --> 00:29:13.640
sharing this with
the marketing firm

00:29:13.640 --> 00:29:16.790
because they might have access
to public data sets that

00:29:16.790 --> 00:29:18.500
contain these
demographic features,

00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:20.499
or they might have their
own internal data sets.

00:29:20.499 --> 00:29:23.740
And we want to stop them
from joining any other data

00:29:23.740 --> 00:29:26.720
sets on this one, or doing
any other kind of thing that

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:28.790
would expose the
user's personal data.

00:29:35.950 --> 00:29:38.970
So first of all, how do we
deal with these outliers,

00:29:38.970 --> 00:29:40.690
and also how do we
measure the risk?

00:29:40.690 --> 00:29:43.390
So one thing we could
do is we could just

00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:45.670
remove all these outliers.

00:29:45.670 --> 00:29:48.790
Just going back to this
previous side for a sec.

00:29:48.790 --> 00:29:51.310
We could drop all these
unique combinations

00:29:51.310 --> 00:29:52.960
of zip code and age.

00:29:52.960 --> 00:29:55.930
But there could
be only two people

00:29:55.930 --> 00:29:58.320
or three people with a given
set of quasi identifiers.

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:00.278
And that's still risky,
even though they're not

00:30:00.278 --> 00:30:01.610
uniquely identifiable.

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:03.950
So to talk about
this kind of risk,

00:30:03.950 --> 00:30:06.160
we're going to use a
concept of k-anonymity.

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:07.600
Which if you're
not familiar with,

00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:08.849
don't worry about it too much.

00:30:08.849 --> 00:30:09.770
It's quite simple.

00:30:09.770 --> 00:30:12.040
It's just how many
people share the same set

00:30:12.040 --> 00:30:13.900
of quasi identifiers--
age and zip code

00:30:13.900 --> 00:30:16.540
in our case-- in
a given data set.

00:30:16.540 --> 00:30:19.125
And so for every possible
combination of age and zip

00:30:19.125 --> 00:30:21.250
code, we're going to count
of the number of people.

00:30:21.250 --> 00:30:23.440
And that's what k-anonymity is.

00:30:23.440 --> 00:30:27.220
And in our case, we're going to
target a policy of k equals 10.

00:30:27.220 --> 00:30:28.990
This could vary from
use case to use case

00:30:28.990 --> 00:30:31.230
depending on who
you're sharing with it,

00:30:31.230 --> 00:30:35.890
and what your assumptions
about the attacker might be,

00:30:35.890 --> 00:30:37.730
and how much risk
you can tolerate.

00:30:37.730 --> 00:30:39.230
But for the purposes
of this demo,

00:30:39.230 --> 00:30:41.646
we're going to aim
for k equals 10.

00:30:41.646 --> 00:30:43.020
And so we're going
to do is we're

00:30:43.020 --> 00:30:45.720
going to take the data
which lives in BigQuery,

00:30:45.720 --> 00:30:48.600
and we're going to call the DLP
API, the risk analysis method,

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:50.310
to compute k-anonymity.

00:30:50.310 --> 00:30:52.140
Then we're going to
take the output, put it

00:30:52.140 --> 00:30:56.361
in Google Data Studio to
visualize the results.

00:30:56.361 --> 00:30:58.110
So if we can switch
back over to the demo.

00:31:00.940 --> 00:31:01.440
OK.

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:04.140
So as you can see,
Scott has pulled up

00:31:04.140 --> 00:31:06.630
the Google API
Explorer, which allows

00:31:06.630 --> 00:31:09.840
you to make API calls to
various Google Cloud Services.

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:15.270
And so here, he has a request up
for the DLP risk analysis API.

00:31:15.270 --> 00:31:16.500
It's fairly simple.

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:19.660
So we're just defining the
quasi identifiers we care about.

00:31:19.660 --> 00:31:21.840
In this case, it's
age and zip code.

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:24.240
And then below, we just
specify the source table,

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:26.580
which is our chats to table.

00:31:26.580 --> 00:31:28.770
And then, again, we're
going to specify an action,

00:31:28.770 --> 00:31:31.380
we want to save the results so
we can generate the histogram,

00:31:31.380 --> 00:31:33.740
and we're going to
save it to this table

00:31:33.740 --> 00:31:37.590
live risk for r1 or x1.

00:31:37.590 --> 00:31:38.954
SCOTT ELLIS: x1.

00:31:38.954 --> 00:31:41.370
MICHAEL DAUB: And then we're
going to give it a unique job

00:31:41.370 --> 00:31:44.310
ID so we can figure out which
job corresponds to the results,

00:31:44.310 --> 00:31:46.530
and then hit execute.

00:31:46.530 --> 00:31:48.510
And hopefully, it worked.

00:31:48.510 --> 00:31:49.620
Yep.

00:31:49.620 --> 00:31:53.260
And so on a larger data
set, it might take longer.

00:31:53.260 --> 00:31:56.190
But this is a test data set,
so it's probably already done.

00:31:56.190 --> 00:31:57.640
It only takes seconds.

00:31:57.640 --> 00:32:00.150
So we're going to switch
over to Google Data Studio

00:32:00.150 --> 00:32:03.240
and open up this report we made.

00:32:03.240 --> 00:32:05.490
And don't worry about how
we generate this report.

00:32:05.490 --> 00:32:07.948
There's a detailed how to guide
that we're going to provide

00:32:07.948 --> 00:32:09.810
at the end of the talk.

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:12.720
But for now, before looking
too much at the graph,

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:15.570
let's talk about what these
axes mean and sort of figure

00:32:15.570 --> 00:32:17.280
out what this graph means.

00:32:17.280 --> 00:32:20.460
So the x-axis, which
is labeled k-anonymity,

00:32:20.460 --> 00:32:23.910
is just, as we talked
about, the number of users

00:32:23.910 --> 00:32:27.030
for each set of values
of quasi identifiers.

00:32:27.030 --> 00:32:29.250
But in this case, we've
aggregated it together

00:32:29.250 --> 00:32:30.510
into a histogram.

00:32:30.510 --> 00:32:32.970
So at the far left, k
equals 1, we've just

00:32:32.970 --> 00:32:37.930
taken everybody who
lives in a zip code--

00:32:37.930 --> 00:32:40.050
a particular age that's
uniquely identifying,

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:43.050
and aggregated them into
one bucket, and said,

00:32:43.050 --> 00:32:45.780
the blue line is
the number of rows--

00:32:45.780 --> 00:32:48.900
or the percentage of total rows
that correspond to k equals 1.

00:32:48.900 --> 00:32:52.440
And the red is the percentage
of unique zip and age

00:32:52.440 --> 00:32:54.880
combinations.

00:32:54.880 --> 00:32:57.240
And so the results
don't look very good,

00:32:57.240 --> 00:33:00.270
because just for k equals
1, already 10% of the data

00:33:00.270 --> 00:33:03.630
is k equals 1 and
27% of the values.

00:33:03.630 --> 00:33:05.160
And it quickly jumps
up to the point

00:33:05.160 --> 00:33:09.690
where once we reach k
equals 9, 100% of our data

00:33:09.690 --> 00:33:12.150
is lost both in terms
of the number of rows

00:33:12.150 --> 00:33:15.779
and the number of distinct
age, zip code combinations.

00:33:15.779 --> 00:33:16.570
So this isn't good.

00:33:16.570 --> 00:33:18.990
We can't just by removing
all of these outliers

00:33:18.990 --> 00:33:24.180
achieve our goal of k equals 10
without losing all of our data.

00:33:24.180 --> 00:33:26.090
And so can we switch
back to the slides?

00:33:30.240 --> 00:33:32.180
So how do we fix this?

00:33:32.180 --> 00:33:35.210
It seems like we're in
an impossible situation

00:33:35.210 --> 00:33:39.850
because we can't remove
the risky outliers,

00:33:39.850 --> 00:33:42.410
but we also need
some data to analyze.

00:33:42.410 --> 00:33:45.080
And so the solution is
to use generalization

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:46.750
using the (DLP) API.

00:33:46.750 --> 00:33:48.710
So generalization is a
technique for, instead

00:33:48.710 --> 00:33:50.540
of just completely
dropping data,

00:33:50.540 --> 00:33:54.330
we're going to replace it with
a value that's not as specific.

00:33:54.330 --> 00:33:58.550
So for example, we might take
ages in a particular range,

00:33:58.550 --> 00:34:01.370
and instead of
outputting the exact age,

00:34:01.370 --> 00:34:04.670
we're going to output
buckets of ages.

00:34:04.670 --> 00:34:07.610
So we might take all
numbers between 20 and 30

00:34:07.610 --> 00:34:12.080
and just output 30, all numbers
30 and 40 and output 40,

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:13.380
and so forth.

00:34:13.380 --> 00:34:15.739
And if we do this,
it will reduce

00:34:15.739 --> 00:34:20.564
the granularity of the
data and reduce the risk.

00:34:20.564 --> 00:34:21.980
And so now, what
we're going to do

00:34:21.980 --> 00:34:24.781
for this final
part of the demo is

00:34:24.781 --> 00:34:26.239
we're going to
combine the last two

00:34:26.239 --> 00:34:32.289
steps where we used Data Flow
to perform de-identification.

00:34:32.289 --> 00:34:34.580
This time, we're going to do
the exact same thing as we

00:34:34.580 --> 00:34:35.996
did before, but
we're going to add

00:34:35.996 --> 00:34:39.080
this bucketing transformation
we didn't do the first time.

00:34:39.080 --> 00:34:41.920
And we're going to bucket
the ages to make it

00:34:41.920 --> 00:34:44.210
a little bit less identifying.

00:34:44.210 --> 00:34:47.330
Then we're going to perform the
second step, take the output,

00:34:47.330 --> 00:34:51.020
pipe it into DLP's
risk analysis API,

00:34:51.020 --> 00:34:53.780
and then once again see
how the results compare

00:34:53.780 --> 00:34:58.160
to the first de-identified--
or the data that did not

00:34:58.160 --> 00:35:01.370
have the bucketing performed.

00:35:01.370 --> 00:35:02.930
And so switch back to the demo.

00:35:05.740 --> 00:35:08.150
And so now we're
going to actually show

00:35:08.150 --> 00:35:11.300
the running of the Data Flow
pipeline, which Scott's just

00:35:11.300 --> 00:35:16.280
going to SSH into a virtual
machine which we've installed

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:18.920
this GitHub library on.

00:35:18.920 --> 00:35:20.990
And the GitHub page has
all the instructions

00:35:20.990 --> 00:35:22.573
on how to run this,
so don't worry too

00:35:22.573 --> 00:35:23.870
much about the exact command.

00:35:23.870 --> 00:35:26.870
But Scott's just
going to prepare it

00:35:26.870 --> 00:35:29.660
by exporting the credentials so
that we're authenticated when

00:35:29.660 --> 00:35:33.400
we actually call Data Flow.

00:35:33.400 --> 00:35:36.800
And so now we're going to run
this command, which, again,

00:35:36.800 --> 00:35:38.180
don't worry too much about it.

00:35:38.180 --> 00:35:43.010
But we're specifying
the input table chats2.

00:35:43.010 --> 00:35:44.750
We're specifying
the output table

00:35:44.750 --> 00:35:49.580
which Scott has called
live_deid_chats2_bucket10_x2.

00:35:49.580 --> 00:35:53.180
And then the
configuration, which

00:35:53.180 --> 00:35:55.490
is the same configuration we
used before, except it has

00:35:55.490 --> 00:35:57.330
the bucketing transformation.

00:35:57.330 --> 00:35:59.520
And so the data
set's pretty small.

00:35:59.520 --> 00:36:04.790
So when Scott runs it, it should
finish probably within seconds.

00:36:04.790 --> 00:36:07.310
For a larger data set, it
might take a bit more time.

00:36:07.310 --> 00:36:10.749
But for this test data
set, it's done pretty fast.

00:36:10.749 --> 00:36:12.290
And so now we're
going to switch over

00:36:12.290 --> 00:36:14.410
to BigQuery real quick
just to look at the output.

00:36:18.380 --> 00:36:21.850
And so first, Scott's
going to pull up the data

00:36:21.850 --> 00:36:25.670
set chats2, which was the
original data set that

00:36:25.670 --> 00:36:27.650
has not yet been de-identified.

00:36:27.650 --> 00:36:30.940
And if we look at it, ages now--

00:36:30.940 --> 00:36:33.820
18, 19, 20, 21, so forth--

00:36:33.820 --> 00:36:35.410
have not been bucketed.

00:36:35.410 --> 00:36:37.900
We've just for the purpose of
this demo dropped the chats,

00:36:37.900 --> 00:36:39.970
but normally, we might
keep the chats in there.

00:36:39.970 --> 00:36:43.565
But just to speed up the
de-identification and Data

00:36:43.565 --> 00:36:44.980
Flow, we remove that.

00:36:44.980 --> 00:36:47.440
And so now if we go
to the output table,

00:36:47.440 --> 00:36:51.280
it's all the same columns,
except now we've applied DID.

00:36:51.280 --> 00:36:53.530
And looking at the
output, now you

00:36:53.530 --> 00:36:56.290
see there are no ages except
for exact multiples of 10

00:36:56.290 --> 00:37:00.190
because we bucketed
them all up together.

00:37:00.190 --> 00:37:01.940
And so now, we're going
to quickly go back

00:37:01.940 --> 00:37:05.340
to the API Explorer and rerun
risk analysis on this output

00:37:05.340 --> 00:37:07.730
data set.

00:37:07.730 --> 00:37:11.210
And so Scott's just going to
copy the table name that we

00:37:11.210 --> 00:37:13.580
just wrote the output to.

00:37:13.580 --> 00:37:15.950
And the API request is
going to be exactly the same

00:37:15.950 --> 00:37:17.584
because we're running
the same metric.

00:37:17.584 --> 00:37:19.250
The only difference
is we want to run it

00:37:19.250 --> 00:37:23.915
on a different output table, the
newly de-identified data set.

00:37:23.915 --> 00:37:26.540
And then we're going to write it
to a slightly different output

00:37:26.540 --> 00:37:30.230
table, and we're also going
to use a different job

00:37:30.230 --> 00:37:33.840
ID because we already
used live risk before X1.

00:37:33.840 --> 00:37:37.822
We're just going to call
it live risk after x1.

00:37:37.822 --> 00:37:39.030
And hopefully this will work.

00:37:44.270 --> 00:37:44.770
All right.

00:37:44.770 --> 00:37:45.292
Good.

00:37:45.292 --> 00:37:47.000
So now, if we switch
back to Data Studio,

00:37:47.000 --> 00:37:50.180
we're going to take the exact
same chart we prepared before.

00:37:50.180 --> 00:37:52.520
But we're just going to
change the data source

00:37:52.520 --> 00:37:57.680
to the new table that we wrote
the output of risk analysis to.

00:37:57.680 --> 00:38:00.710
So if-- Scott's just going
to click Edit and then

00:38:00.710 --> 00:38:06.710
change the data source from the
original data set to the one

00:38:06.710 --> 00:38:09.210
we just created.

00:38:09.210 --> 00:38:11.766
And once that's done, the
graph will be updated.

00:38:11.766 --> 00:38:13.890
And we're going to see what
the results of applying

00:38:13.890 --> 00:38:16.570
this bucketing was on the risk.

00:38:16.570 --> 00:38:19.530
So it's disappearing now.

00:38:19.530 --> 00:38:22.440
But if you recall, it
started out relatively high,

00:38:22.440 --> 00:38:23.940
and then quickly
jumped up in terms

00:38:23.940 --> 00:38:27.060
of the amount of
data loss experienced

00:38:27.060 --> 00:38:30.462
by removing the outliers.

00:38:30.462 --> 00:38:31.920
But this looks much
better already,

00:38:31.920 --> 00:38:35.020
because if you look at k
equals 1, it's kind of hidden,

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:36.630
but it says 0.3%.

00:38:36.630 --> 00:38:40.380
Only 0.3% of all rows
correspond to k equals 1

00:38:40.380 --> 00:38:42.150
after applying this bucketing.

00:38:42.150 --> 00:38:47.790
And only 5% of the values are
lost if we want to k equals 1.

00:38:47.790 --> 00:38:50.660
And it increases
very slowly up to k

00:38:50.660 --> 00:38:53.010
equals 8, the data
loss, and only starts

00:38:53.010 --> 00:38:55.734
jumping up when we
hit k equals 15.

00:38:55.734 --> 00:38:57.525
And we can get actually
all the way up to k

00:38:57.525 --> 00:38:59.580
equals 40 before we
lose all of our data.

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:02.790
But since our goal was k
equals 10, this was a success.

00:39:02.790 --> 00:39:06.540
We can hit k equals 10 and
only lose about 4% of the rows

00:39:06.540 --> 00:39:08.590
and about 20% of
our distinct values.

00:39:11.900 --> 00:39:13.160
So switch back to the slides.

00:39:17.487 --> 00:39:20.070
So I'm just going to skip over
that, because we already showed

00:39:20.070 --> 00:39:22.400
you the graph that was back up.

00:39:22.400 --> 00:39:26.380
So let's just look at the
technical details real quick.

00:39:26.380 --> 00:39:29.290
We use the exact same DID to
config we showed you earlier,

00:39:29.290 --> 00:39:31.810
only we added this
extra bucketing config

00:39:31.810 --> 00:39:35.050
for applying the bucketing
transformation to the age

00:39:35.050 --> 00:39:37.780
column.

00:39:37.780 --> 00:39:40.510
And it's actually a
very flexible API.

00:39:40.510 --> 00:39:44.170
We just say what the min value
and the max value of the range

00:39:44.170 --> 00:39:45.550
for the bucketing is.

00:39:45.550 --> 00:39:47.710
And in this case, we defined
the replacement value

00:39:47.710 --> 00:39:49.340
to be the upper end
of the range 30.

00:39:49.340 --> 00:39:51.340
But you could define it
to be whatever you want.

00:39:51.340 --> 00:39:55.810
You could make it a string that
is just 20-30, sort of gives

00:39:55.810 --> 00:39:56.422
a range.

00:39:56.422 --> 00:39:57.880
You could choose
the lower endpoint

00:39:57.880 --> 00:40:00.850
20, the midpoint 25, the
upper end 30, or pretty much

00:40:00.850 --> 00:40:01.630
whatever you want.

00:40:05.880 --> 00:40:10.780
And so quick summary of what
we just showed you is first,

00:40:10.780 --> 00:40:15.070
Scott showed you how to scan
your Google Cloud Storage

00:40:15.070 --> 00:40:19.390
bucket for sensitive data
and generate an audit report.

00:40:19.390 --> 00:40:23.980
Then he showed you how to
in real time de-identify

00:40:23.980 --> 00:40:28.360
the chat messages so that
analysts didn't have to see PII

00:40:28.360 --> 00:40:32.740
and so that we weren't storing
the sensitive data we didn't

00:40:32.740 --> 00:40:34.300
need.

00:40:34.300 --> 00:40:36.610
And then we showed
you in the second half

00:40:36.610 --> 00:40:40.570
how to de-identify a data
set using Data Flow and share

00:40:40.570 --> 00:40:42.850
with an internal team,
and then use risk analysis

00:40:42.850 --> 00:40:45.760
to make sure it's safe for
sharing with an external team.

00:40:45.760 --> 00:40:49.110
